  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 117 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft continues pumping more monsoonal moisture into \nour area. Today will have the highest rainfall chances of the week \nas an embedded shortwave moves through overhead. This will bring a \npush of moisture set to arrive to our forecast area this afternoon \nand evening. In fact, showers and thunderstorms are already ongoing \nacross the Four Corners region and Southwest CO as of 1pm. At the \nsame time, a jet streak is departing the region to the east putting \nus in the favorable right entrance region this afternoon. It is \nimportant to note that SPC mesoscale analysis currently shows \ninstability lacking across our area, which could limit the severe \npotential, especially for the I-25 urban corridor and adjacent \nplains.\n\nThe main concern with storms today will be gusty and erratic outflow \nwinds as well as microbursts capable of producing wind gusts of 40 \nto 50 mph as DCAPE is expected to be around 1000 J/kg. MLCAPE is \nforecast to reach around 1000 J/kg, mainly for the far eastern \nplains. 0-6 km bulk shear should be about 30 to 40 kts with the \ngreatest shear expected in our eastern plains where temperatures \nhave warmed into the mid 80s as of 12pm. Therefore, a low risk for \nhail of 1 to perhaps 1.5 inches in diameter exists for the far \neastern plains. There is also a low flood threat given that the \ningredients are in place, but storm motion should be rather quick \nthis afternoon with mean wind around 30-40 kts- limiting the overall \nthreat. A decent moist vertical profile for the area means PWATs of \n150-175% of average for this time of year. Thus, burn scars, areas \nwhere storms linger, and locations that receive multiple rounds of \nheavy rainfall today would be most susceptible to minor flooding. \nThe warm cloud layer depth is relatively shallow, further limiting \nthe flooding threat.\n\nCAMs are in decent agreement that there will be at least two rounds \nof showers and storms this afternoon and evening. Showers and storms \nshould overspread the southern high country as early as 2-3pm. The \nmost widespread showers for the high country and Urban Corridor \nshould be between about 4pm and 8pm. These should then push off to \nthe north and east. An additional round or two of showers and storms \nis possible for the evening and overnight hours through about 2am \ntomorrow morning. Tonight, some low clouds and patchy fog are \npossible for the plains following the rain. Some light rain showers \ncould linger into tomorrow morning for the plains, though the odds \nare rather low. \n\nHigh temperatures for today and tomorrow will be near average- in \nthe mid 80s for the plains. Some isolated storms are possible for \nthe high country and areas south and east of Denver tomorrow \nafternoon. There will likely be a low threat for severe weather near \nthe Palmer Divide with any storms that could form, but moisture and \nlift will be lacking compared to today. The high pressure shifts off \nto the mid-Atlantic and Southern US shutting off the monsoonal \nmoisture feed into our area by Wednesday. High temperatures will \nreturn to above average- in the mid 80s and low 90s across the \nplains- for the end of the week and into the weekend. Little to no \nrainfall is expected through at least Friday.\n\nBy the weekend, south to southwest flow aloft could return. Moisture \nfrom a tropical disturbance in the Pacific could also be pulled into \nthe Western US, but as of now the deepest moisture looks to miss our \narea to the west. Thus, the highest rainfall chances should stay \nconfined to the high country until Sunday or Monday.",
